  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>(i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><msqrt><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></msqrt></math>. Clearly <math> <mrow> <msub> <mi>f</mi> <mn>1</mn> </msub> <mrow><mo>(</mo> <mi>x</mi> <mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow> </math> is continuous at <math> <mrow> <mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow> </math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mn>0</mn></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>&#8242;</mi></msubsup><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mn>0</mn></mfenced><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><msqrt><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></msqrt></mrow><msqrt><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></msqrt></mfrac><mo>&#183;</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></msqrt><mo>&#183;</mo><mfrac><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#183;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#183;</mo><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfrac></math>, which does not exist. So it is&nbsp;not differentiable at&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>.<br />So, <math><mi>P</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>2</mn></math><br />(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"\" open=\"{\" separators=\"|\"><mtable><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\" separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8800;</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mfenced></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>x</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mrow><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> and <math> <mrow> <munder> <mrow> <mi>lim</mi></mrow> <mrow> <mi>x</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><msup> <mn>0</mn> <mo>&#8722;</mo> </msup> </mrow> </munder> <mo>&#8722;</mo><mfrac> <mrow> <mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow> <mi>x</mi> </mfrac> <mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac> <mi>x</mi> <mrow> <msup> <mrow> <mi>tan</mi></mrow> <mrow> <mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow> </msup> <mi>x</mi></mrow> </mfrac> <mo>=</mo><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow> </math><br /><math><mo>&#8658;</mo></math> <math><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> is not continuous at <math><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br />So <math><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>1</mn></math><br />(iii) In the close neighborhood of <math> <mrow> <mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow> </math> given function <math> <mrow> <msub> <mi>f</mi> <mn>3</mn> </msub> <mrow><mo>(</mo> <mi>x</mi> <mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow> </math> hence <math> <mrow> <msub> <msup> <mi>f</mi> <mo>&#8242;</mo> </msup> <mn>3</mn> </msub> <mrow><mo>(</mo> <mi>x</mi> <mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow> </math> also <math> <mrow> <msub> <msup> <mi>f</mi> <mo>&#8242;</mo> </msup> <mn>3</mn> </msub> <mrow><mo>(</mo> <mi>x</mi> <mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow> </math> is continuous at <math> <mrow> <mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow> </math><br />So, <math><mi>R</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>4</mn></math><br />(iv) <math><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"\" open=\"{\" separators=\"|\"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd><msup><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin</mi></mrow><mo>&#8289;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8800;</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>Although&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#39;</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mn>0</mn></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>&nbsp;due to sandwich theorem, but&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#39;</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mi>x</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;does not exist because&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cos</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mfenced></math>&nbsp;oscillates infinitely many times near&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>.<br />So <math><mi>S</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p>",
